## ─── Nodes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## EFFICIENCY guard (deterministic, no LLM) AND the scope's single source of
## truth: resolve the base once, then compute the review diff against it —
## the same diff the reviewers audit, because they are handed the same sha.
## If it is empty there is nothing to review, so short-circuit past both LLM
## reviewers + emit and finish fast. Only skips when the merge-base resolves
## AND the diff is empty; on any ambiguity (e.g. base_ref absent) it runs
## the reviewers — never wrongly skips a real review.

dsl: 2

## The CLAUDE-FAMILY merge step, taken by every review whose launch does not
## carry `mono_family=gpt` (`merge_reviews -> converge_gpt when
## vars.mono_family == 'gpt'` is the guard, and `converge` is the
## unconditional fallback beside it — the same shape as `topology`'s reviewer
## edges).
##
## The guard reads that ONE var, so it follows the launch's pin and not the
## topology the run ends up with: a `review_tier: audit` launch, or an
## explicit `--var review_mode=dual`, carrying `mono_family=gpt` fans BOTH
## reviewers and still merges on the twin. Deliberate — that pin is the
## operator's explicit choice, and on a GPT-only tenant (where InjectAll
## injects it) putting the merge back on the Anthropic wire is the very
## failure #2467 closes. A webhook launch reaches this node whenever the
## publisher resolved dual, because reviewtopology.InjectIfDeclaredFamilies
## deletes `mono_family` there and the var falls back to its declared
## "claude" default.
agent converge:
  ## Dedicated env (NOT ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE, which pins the
  ## REVIEWER): the merge is near-mechanical — dedupe, threshold, cap and
  ## report. Opus 5.5 at medium effort is the Anthropic default; this
  ## node's model and provider dials move it to any model a CLI backend
  ## serves (the backend dial is bounded — see there), and under
  ## `mono_family=gpt` the twin below answers instead, on its own three.
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_EMIT:-claude-opus-5-5}"
  ## Overridable like every other route here, because this node is on
  ## every path the gpt guard does not take. Pinned to one backend, it made
  ## the gpt family a half promise — the review ran, then the merge died on
  ## the Anthropic weekly cap, so a deployment whose Claude window is spent
  ## could not review at all despite having a second family credentialed.
  ##
  ## BETWEEN CLI BACKENDS ONLY — claude_code, codex, pi, kimi, grok,
  ## opencode. This node declares no `tools:`, which a CLI backend reads as
  ## "no restriction" and claw as the node's WHOLE surface, so pointed at
  ## claw the dial lands the merge with no tool at all: no report under
  ## `report_path`, no board issue, and the capability grant below inert —
  ## every append is gated on an already-restricted list
  ## (model.assembleEffectiveTools). Declaring a list here is not the
  ## answer either: on a CLI route the list becomes the whole surface too
  ## and strips the shell this node's own boundary paragraph names
  ## (TestReviewPrSharedPromptsStateEachRoutesSurface). The claw merge is
  ## therefore a node of its own — `converge_gpt` declares the list, and
  ## `mono_family=gpt` selects it.
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_EMIT:-claude_code}"
  ## Same Anthropic-direct pin as the claude slot, for the same reason
  ## (see reviewer_claude): unpinned, a capped forfait hands this node's
  ## claude id to the z.ai facade in silence. Its own dial, because this
  ## node's backend is one too: moving the slot to another CLI backend's
  ## model needs this hint moved with it — a hint names the route to the
  ## credential walk and the wire-reach check, so a leftover `anthropic`
  ## would describe an Anthropic route this node no longer takes. An
  ## `openai/…` merge is the twin below, not a hint here: the twin pins claw
  ## for that id (see there), the one route this node's `tools:`-less
  ## surface cannot take.
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_EMIT:-anthropic}"
  output: emit_output
  ## The merged finding set a later run is handed, kept beside pr_gate's
  ## own publication (manifest `produces:` names pr_gate, because it is the
  ## one node BOTH twins feed): an artifact is written ONLY for a node
  ## carrying `publish:`, and this one is this twin's own recovery, which
  ## the hand-off consults when the gate's copy cannot be read.
  publish: review_findings
  system: emit_system
  user: emit_user
  ## board.read to load the iterion-board skill, board.create +
  ## board.label to file findings. emit writes the report file via its
  ## ordinary claude_code tools.
  capabilities: [board.read, board.create, board.label]
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_EMIT:-medium}"
  ## Converge the two parallel reviewer branches: emit runs once, after
  ## BOTH reviewers complete, and reads their outputs directly.
  await: wait_all
  fallbacks:
    ## The claude slot's GLM route, verbatim and on the same dial (see
    ## reviewer_claude for every trigger and for why it carries no
    ## `backend:`): this node is on every path the gpt guard does not take,
    ## so a merge step with no second server let a capped forfait cost the
    ## whole review even when the reviewer itself had been rescued.
    ## claude_code only — on a claw primary the hint is read nowhere and a
    ## bare GLM id is not a claw spec, so the route fails fast.
    glm:
      model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE_FALLBACK:-glm-5.3}"
      provider: "zai"
      on: [usage_window, auth, unavailable]
      metered: true

## The GPT-FAMILY merge step (#2467). `mono_family=gpt` is resolved from the
## run's credentials and claude is the preferred family
## (reviewtopology.monoPreference), so a run carrying it has NO Claude
## credential: a merge pinned to the Anthropic wire then dies after the
## reviewer has already spent, and the run never reaches `pr_gate` — the
## merge gate stays dark on a PR nobody can see was reviewed. A node's
## `model:`/`backend:` resolve `${ENV:-default}` and never `{{vars.x}}`, so
## the family cannot be a var here: it is a second, differently pinned node
## behind the same guard that picks the reviewer (the glance tiers' shape).
##
## Everything but the route is SHARED with `converge` by name — the same
## prompts, output schema, effort dial and capabilities — so the merge
## contract has exactly one copy
## (bots/review_pr_mono_gpt_merge_test.go).
agent converge_gpt:
  ## The OpenAI merge model this bundle documents (README, "Running
  ## mono-GPT"). Its own three dials, moving TOGETHER like the gpt
  ## reviewer's: backend, model and provider decide one route, and a
  ## deployment that moves one of them alone lands the slot somewhere
  ## nobody chose. The `_EMIT` trio keeps dialling the claude twin.
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_EMIT_GPT:-openai/gpt-6-sol}"
  ## Explicit pin: an `openai/…` id only resolves via claw (the
  ## auto-resolver would send it to claude_code, which does not know the
  ## prefix, and the schema validator fails).
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_EMIT_GPT:-claw}"
  ## Empty by default, like the gpt reviewer's: on claw the model's
  ## `openai/` prefix IS the route, while a leftover hint would describe an
  ## Anthropic route this node does not take to the credential walk and to
  ## the wire-reach check (model.AnthropicWireRoutes).
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_EMIT_GPT:-}"
  output: emit_output
  ## Its OWN artifact name, not the claude twin's: the hand-off reads a
  ## publication by NODE id (manifest `fallback_nodes`), so the name buys
  ## nothing here while a shared one would make two nodes contend for the
  ## same logical alias when a resume reconciles artifact owners.
  publish: review_findings_gpt
  system: emit_system
  user: emit_user
  ## The node's WHOLE surface on claw, where an empty `tools:` list means
  ## no tools at all — not "no restriction" as it does on claude_code,
  ## which hands the claude twin its full native toolset. These three carry
  ## the merge's two side effects: `read_file` loads the iterion-board
  ## skill, `write_file` writes the report under `report_path` (it creates
  ## parent dirs), `glob` is how claw lists a directory. The board tools
  ## ride the capability append on top, under the `mcp__iterion_board__`
  ## names the prompt calls.
  tools: [read_file, write_file, glob]
  ## Same three as the claude twin, and reached here only because the
  ## `tools:` list above is non-empty: the board MCP append is gated on an
  ## already-restricted surface (model.assembleEffectiveTools), so on claw
  ## the capability without the list would grant nothing.
  capabilities: [board.read, board.create, board.label]
  ## claw bounds a declared tool loop at 5 steps when the node names no
  ## number (claw_backend), while the audit tier lets this step file 40
  ## board issues before it writes the report: at the default the merge
  ## would stop mid-publication.
  tool_max_steps: 60
  ## SHARED with the claude twin, deliberately: the published run table
  ## reads the merge step's effort from the ENVIRONMENT (publish_review's
  ## AI_CONVERGE_EFFORT), not from the node that ran, so a second dial here
  ## would print a number the run did not use.
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_EMIT:-medium}"
  ## Same convergence as the claude twin: one firing, after both reviewer
  ## branches have settled.
  await: wait_all
  ## No rescue route, like every other gpt node here: the GLM route is
  ## claude_code's (it carries a `provider:` hint, which claw reads
  ## nowhere), and a deployment without a usable OpenAI route moves this
  ## slot's three dials instead.

judge reviewer_claude:
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE:-claude-opus-5-5}"
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_CLAUDE:-claude_code}"
  ## The `anthropic` hint is LOAD-BEARING, not decorative: the wire's
  ## credential order (secrets.AnthropicWireSlotOrder) puts facade keys
  ## (zai, moonshot) FIRST, and a z.ai key takes the wire as the run's
  ## DEFAULT whenever the forfait is skipped — the publisher skips a
  ## forfait whose window is closed (forfaitWindowClosed: a provider
  ## refusal, or an operator usage cap, soft or hard) and falls through to
  ## the next tier. Unpinned, this node would then send
  ## its claude id to the z.ai facade, which ACCEPTS it and serves GLM in
  ## silence, under the claude label, with the GLM route below never
  ## firing. Pinned, the primary spends the sealed Anthropic credential,
  ## else the runner's ambient Anthropic auth, else fails "Not logged in"
  ## and the route takes it, stamped `_fallback_used` and `glm-5.3`.
  ## The hint forces Anthropic-direct: the anthropic key if sealed, else
  ## the claude_code forfait — skipping the facade branches entirely
  ## (selectedAnthropicCredEnvForCLI). A dial, one for both tiers of the
  ## slot, so an operator can put the slot on z.ai deliberately
  ## (ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_CLAUDE=zai) without re-releasing the bot.
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_CLAUDE:-anthropic}"
  output: review_output
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_claude
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high}"
  ## `readonly:` is a SCHEDULING declaration — the node mutates no
  ## workspace file, which is what lets dual fan both reviewers onto one
  ## worktree — and a tool boundary, with the lever each backend has:
  ## codex an OS sandbox, pi a readers-only tool list, claude_code the
  ## withholding of the file-writing tools whatever this node's `tools:`
  ## list says (here it declares none at all), plus the pair that moves
  ## the worktree; claw, opencode and cliagent do not read the flag at
  ## all, so there the `tools:` list is the only bound. It is not a
  ## sandbox on claude_code either: the native shell stays, so the
  ## prompt and the output schema still carry the rest.
  ## Declaring `tools:` here would bound that shell too, and is not free
  ## — a non-empty list installs --disallowedTools and takes
  ## Task/WebFetch/Skill off this node on every run.
  readonly: true
  fallbacks:
    ## GLM, the claude family's other server, when the Anthropic route
    ## pinned above cannot serve. Without it the operator's only recourse
    ## was to point the GPT slot's model at GLM — spending the
    ## cross-family check to buy capacity, on the bot whose product IS
    ## that check.
    ##
    ## No `backend:` on purpose: the route stays on claude_code, which is
    ## the ONE backend that honours a provider hint
    ## (providerFallbackEligible). `provider: zai` then forces the z.ai
    ## facade — ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL + ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN from the run's
    ## zai credential — even when Anthropic credentials are present.
    ## Routing this to claw instead would read the hint nowhere, resolve
    ## `anthropic/…` back to the credential that just failed, and cross a
    ## backend boundary that costs this node an explicit tools: list —
    ## which on claude_code is NOT inert, it installs --disallowedTools
    ## and takes Task/WebFetch/Skill off the PRIMARY path. The hint keeps
    ## the whole thing on one backend. (claw DOES reach GLM its own way —
    ## model `anthropic/glm-5.3`, provider read from the spec prefix, as
    ## the converge dial's OpenAI alternative documents — that is just
    ## not this route.)
    ##
    ## What each trigger is FOR — the primary is forced Anthropic-direct,
    ## so every one of these is a failure OF THAT ROUTE (a sealed z.ai
    ## key plays no part in it):
    ##   usage_window — the forfait's usage window is spent. The
    ##     production case: the weekly cap diverts the review to GLM
    ##     instead of parking it.
    ##   auth        — the Anthropic credential present but rejected or
    ##     expired: it still takes precedence, so the CLI uses it and
    ##     401s. Not in the default trigger set, named here on purpose:
    ##     the shipped default sends auth to a human because re-auth is
    ##     usually the cure, and parking the review gate while a second
    ##     credentialed route is DECLARED is the limitation, not the
    ##     safety. (AuthFailedRecipe still catches what the chain cannot
    ##     serve — the chain is tried first, not bypassed.) An instance
    ##     with ONLY a z.ai key lands here too: the forced-Anthropic
    ##     primary has no Anthropic channel, fails auth, and this route
    ##     takes it.
    ##   unavailable — the resolved credential cannot reach the model.
    ## With NO credential at all the route has none either, and the run
    ## fails loudly, which is correct.
    ## Nothing is silent: `_backend`/`_model` name what served and the
    ## published review's run table carries them.
    ##
    ## One dial for both tiers, because the rescue is deliberately the
    ## same on both (see reviewer_claude_glance). An account entitled to
    ## a different GLM id — or one z.ai renames — repoints it without
    ## re-releasing the bot; element models are ${VAR:-default}-expanded
    ## at dispatch like the node's own.
    glm:
      model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE_FALLBACK:-glm-5.3}"
      provider: "zai"
      on: [usage_window, auth, unavailable]
      metered: true

judge reviewer_gpt:
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT:-openai/gpt-6-astra}"
  ## Explicit backend pin: "openai/gpt-6-astra" only resolves via claw.
  ## Without it the auto-resolver routes through claude_code, which
  ## doesn't recognise openai/* models and the schema validator fails.
  ## Backend, model AND provider are dials, so an instance points the
  ## second reviewer at whatever its credentials serve — all three move
  ## together, or the slot lands on a route nobody chose. The glance tier
  ## has its own backend and model dials (the _GLANCE ones) and shares the
  ## provider one, so a remedy moves both tiers:
  ##   claude family: ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_GPT and _GPT_GLANCE=claude_code,
  ##     ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT and _GPT_GLANCE=claude-opus-5-5,
  ##     ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_GPT=anthropic (without the hint a z.ai key
  ##     holding the wire answers the claude id with GLM — see
  ##     reviewer_claude);
  ##   GLM: ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_GPT and _GPT_GLANCE=claude_code,
  ##     ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT and _GPT_GLANCE=glm-5.3,
  ##     ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_GPT=zai.
  ## The provider dial is empty by default: on claw the model's
  ## `openai/` prefix is the route.
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_GPT:-claw}"
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_GPT:-}"
  output: review_output
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_gpt
  ## Read-only toolset only (no write_file / file_edit): the claw
  ## reviewer can run git + read the workspace but has no structured
  ## path to edit it.
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high}"

## GLANCE-TIER reviewers (#685). The "cheaper reviewer model" lever cannot
## be expressed as a var on reviewer_claude/reviewer_gpt above: a node's
## `model:`/`reasoning_effort:` only ever resolves `${ENV_VAR:-default}`
## from the process environment, never `{{vars.x}}` — true on EVERY
## backend, claw included (a template reaches the CLI/claw call literally
## and fails). So a runtime-selectable model needs a second, differently
## pinned node, picked by the SAME `topology` router that already picks
## the family — never a new engine branch. Same prompts/schema/read-only
## posture as their full-strength counterparts; only the model/effort
## differ, and only topology's edges decide which of the two ever runs.
judge reviewer_claude_glance:
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE_GLANCE:-claude-opus-5-5}"
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_CLAUDE_GLANCE:-claude_code}"
  ## Same Anthropic-direct pin and dial as reviewer_claude — the glance
  ## tier must not resolve onto a facade key either.
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_CLAUDE:-anthropic}"
  output: review_output
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_claude
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE_GLANCE:-medium}"
  readonly: true
  fallbacks:
    ## The glance tier is a cheaper PRIMARY, not a cheaper rescue: this
    ## route reads the SAME dial as reviewer_claude's, because a smaller
    ## z.ai id is not one this repo has measured, and a rescue that
    ## guesses a model id fails on the spec instead of the credential.
    ## See reviewer_claude for why the route carries no `backend:`.
    glm:
      model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_CLAUDE_FALLBACK:-glm-5.3}"
      provider: "zai"
      on: [usage_window, auth, unavailable]
      metered: true

judge reviewer_gpt_glance:
  model: "${ITERION_VIBE_MODEL_GPT_GLANCE:-openai/gpt-6-luna}"
  ## Same three dials as reviewer_gpt (see there); the provider one is
  ## shared, since it follows the family, not the tier.
  backend: "${ITERION_VIBE_BACKEND_GPT_GLANCE:-claw}"
  provider: "${ITERION_VIBE_PROVIDER_GPT:-}"
  output: review_output
  system: review_system
  user: review_user_gpt
  tools: [bash, read_file, glob, grep]
  tool_max_steps: 30
  reasoning_effort: "${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT_GLANCE:-medium}"

## Topology gate. A `condition` router picks a SINGLE target, which is
## exactly what mono needs; the guard cannot live on `fan`'s own edges
## because fan_out_all collects every outgoing edge without evaluating
## `when` (same trap as round_robin).
router topology:
  mode: condition

router fan:
  mode: fan_out_all

tool diff_precheck:
  command: `WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} BASE={{vars.base_ref}} python3 -I -c "
import os, subprocess, json
os.chdir(os.environ['WS'])
def git(*a):
    try:
        return subprocess.run(['git', *a], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120, env=dict(os.environ, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT='0'))
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return subprocess.CompletedProcess(['git', *a], 1, '', 'timed out')
base = os.environ.get('BASE', 'main')
head = git('rev-parse', 'HEAD').stdout.strip()
# A review workspace must equal the tree under review: a TRACKED file with
# an uncommitted edit means it does not — an edit an earlier run left
# behind, or a generator whose output differs from the committed file —
# and git-diff against the base (the reviewers' scope) reads the working
# tree, so that edit would be filed as a PR finding (#1666). Named here so
# the reviewers scope it OUT and the publish step routes it as a workspace
# anomaly. The read lists tracked files only (untracked entries never
# enter that diff — and this bot's own .review-pr/ scratch is untracked by
# design) and excludes the tree noise the engine and the tooling write
# (the .claude mirror the engine rewrites, devbox.lock, script scratch),
# which is not the workspace drifting either. Empty in base_ref: HEAD
# mode — uncommitted work IS the object there, no anomaly. (No backticks
# in these comments: the command string is backtick-delimited.)
#
# Read in -z: the line form C-quotes any name with a space or a non-ASCII
# byte (quotepath), and the publish step's strip matches the finding's
# plain path — a quoted name would slip PAST it and be posted as a PR
# finding. NUL-terminated fields are never quoted. A rename/copy is TWO
# fields (new, then old); the old path is skipped — a finding anchors to
# the new one. The entries travel newline-joined to the publish step (the
# scalar-substitution contract), so a name containing a literal newline
# degrades to its FIRST line as the anomaly entry (the orphan fragment is
# rejected at the publish parse) — such a file's own drift still escapes
# the strip, an accepted residual. A FAILED read emits the '?' marker
# rather than a clean tree: an empty value would disarm the publish
# step's strip in silence, and the review would read as if the workspace
# had been verified.
noise = os.environ.get('ITERION_TREE_NOISE', '').split()
porcelain = git('status', '--porcelain', '-z', '--untracked-files=no', '--', ':/', *noise)
workspace_anomaly = ''
if base != 'HEAD':
    if porcelain.returncode != 0:
        workspace_anomaly = '?'
    else:
        anomaly_entries = []
        fields = porcelain.stdout.split('\0')
        i = 0
        while i < len(fields):
            entry = fields[i]
            i += 1
            if not entry:
                continue
            anomaly_entries.append(entry)
            if entry[0] in ('R', 'C'):
                i += 1
        workspace_anomaly = chr(10).join(anomaly_entries)
base_is_current = False
if base != 'HEAD' and git('fetch', '--quiet', 'origin', base).returncode == 0:
    fetched = git('rev-parse', 'FETCH_HEAD')
    if fetched.returncode == 0 and fetched.stdout.strip():
        base = fetched.stdout.strip()
        base_is_current = True
def unresolved(b):
    print(json.dumps({'is_empty': False, 'changed_files': -1, 'reviewed_sha': head, 'base_sha': b, 'base_is_current': False, 'workspace_anomaly': workspace_anomaly})); raise SystemExit(0)
mb = git('merge-base', base, 'HEAD').stdout.strip()
if not mb:
    unresolved(base)
diff = git('diff', '--name-only', mb)
if diff.returncode != 0:
    unresolved(mb)
files = [f for f in diff.stdout.strip().splitlines() if f.strip()]
print(json.dumps({'is_empty': len(files) == 0, 'changed_files': len(files), 'reviewed_sha': head, 'base_sha': mb, 'base_is_current': base_is_current, 'workspace_anomaly': workspace_anomaly}))
"`
  output: diff_precheck_output
  ## Carries reviewed_sha, the revision the findings anchor to, and
  ## base_sha — the ONE resolved base every consumer diffs against.
  ##
  ## The base is refreshed from the remote before the merge-base is taken.
  ## A checkout reused across runs carries whatever `base_ref` meant when it
  ## was made, and merge-base against a stale base lands BELOW the branch
  ## point: the diff then contains every file merged into the base since,
  ## and the review reports on code the branch never touched, as if it had.
  ## Measured 2026-09-15: a review scoped itself onto five files of a pull
  ## request merged three days earlier and emitted a blocking finding with
  ## no line in the real diff to anchor to.
  ##
  ## Refreshing is best-effort by design — no remote, no network and an
  ## opaque `base_ref` are all legitimate — so a failure degrades to the
  ## local ref and says so through base_is_current rather than failing the
  ## run. That degradation only holds if the call RETURNS, hence the bounded
  ## timeout and GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0: a remote that blackholes packets or
  ## asks for credentials would otherwise park the workflow's entry node
  ## until max_duration, and a review that never reaches its publish step
  ## leaves the required check `pending` for good. `base_ref: HEAD` (the
  ## review-uncommitted-only mode) is never fetched; there is nothing to
  ## refresh.
  ##
  ## EVERY way of not knowing the scope emits the same sentinel —
  ## `is_empty: false` with `changed_files: -1` — because the two ways of
  ## reading "no files" are a whole review apart: "the branch changed
  ## nothing", which legitimately skips the reviewers, and "the question
  ## could not be answered", which must never skip them. A git call that
  ## fails or times out returns empty stdout, so a result parsed without
  ## checking its return code is indistinguishable from an empty diff, and
  ## short-circuits to a green gate on a pull request nobody read.
  ##
  ## The unresolved paths: the merge-base does not resolve (unrelated
  ## histories, a shallow clone, an unresolvable base_ref), or the diff
  ## itself fails. In both, base_sha is the base that was TRIED, never HEAD
  ## — the reviewers diff against base_sha, and a diff against HEAD is empty
  ## on a clean checkout, so HEAD would hand them silence instead of an
  ## error. base_is_current is forced false so the prompt's
  ## unresolved-scope rule fires.
  publish: reviewed_revision

## DETERMINISTIC forge publish (no LLM, no workspace credential): map the
## merged findings onto the iterion server's forge-publish payload and
## POST it to {{vars.forge_publish_url}} with the per-run token. The
## SERVER anchors the inline comments and renders the per-forge one-click
## suggestion syntax through the team connection's LIVE forge client, so
## a frozen workspace token can never 401 this step (prod run 019f8861's
## failure mode). Any failure is reported honestly in skipped_reason —
## published is true ONLY on a 2xx from the endpoint, whose counts are
## forge-re-fetched (anti-façade), never self-estimated.
tool publish_review:
  command: `AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_EFFORT=${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE:-high} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_EFFORT=${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT:-high} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_EFFORT=${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_CLAUDE_GLANCE:-medium} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE_EFFORT=${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_GPT_GLANCE:-medium} AI_CONVERGE_EFFORT=${ITERION_VIBE_EFFORT_EMIT:-medium} AI_RUN_ID={{run.id}} AI_RUN_TOKENS={{input.ai_run_tokens}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_MODEL={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_model}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_BACKEND={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_backend}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_TOKENS={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_tokens}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_FALLBACK={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_fallback}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_WIRE={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_wire}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_MODEL={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_model}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_BACKEND={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_backend}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_TOKENS={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_tokens}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_WIRE={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_wire}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_MODEL={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_glance_model}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_BACKEND={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_glance_backend}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_TOKENS={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_glance_tokens}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_FALLBACK={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_glance_fallback}} AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_WIRE={{input.ai_reviewer_claude_glance_wire}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE_MODEL={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_model}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE_BACKEND={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_backend}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE_TOKENS={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_tokens}} AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE_WIRE={{input.ai_reviewer_gpt_glance_wire}} AI_CONVERGE_MODEL={{input.ai_converge_model}} AI_CONVERGE_BACKEND={{input.ai_converge_backend}} AI_CONVERGE_TOKENS={{input.ai_converge_tokens}} AI_CONVERGE_FALLBACK={{input.ai_converge_fallback}} AI_CONVERGE_WIRE={{input.ai_converge_wire}} REVIEW_SCOPE={{input.review_scope}} WS={{vars.workspace_dir}} REVIEWED_SHA={{input.reviewed_sha}} PUB_URL={{vars.forge_publish_url}} PUB_TOKEN={{vars.forge_publish_token}} MODE={{vars.pr_review_mode}} REVIEW_MODE={{input.effective_review_mode}} PR_URL={{input.pr_url}} FINDINGS={{input.findings}} QUESTIONS={{input.questions}} TICKET_CONFORMANCE={{input.ticket_conformance}} CLAUDE_FINDINGS={{input.claude_findings}} GPT_FINDINGS={{input.gpt_findings}} GATE_ENABLED={{vars.gate_enabled}} GATE_SEVERITY={{vars.gate_severity}} GATE_CONTEXT={{vars.gate_context}} FIXER_HINT={{vars.fixer_hint}} SCOPE_FILES={{input.scope_files}} WORKSPACE_ANOMALY={{input.workspace_anomaly}} python3 -I -c "
import os, json, subprocess, urllib.request, urllib.error, hashlib, re

# Stable handle for one finding, shared with iterion's prior-review digest
# (pkg/server/webhooks_handoff.go findingID) and with the fixer's ledger:
# the operator arbitrates by it (/billy skip R7a3f) and the fixer reports by it.
# Keyed on (file, normalized title) and NOT on the line, because the line moves
# as soon as code above it changes -- which is exactly what a fix does, so a
# line-keyed id would break over the single round-trip it exists for.
# bots/review_pr_finding_id_test.go pins this against the Go derivation.
def finding_id(f):
    t = ' '.join(str(f.get('title') or '').split()).lower()[:80]
    key = str(f.get('file') or '').strip() + chr(10) + t
    return 'R' + hashlib.sha256(key.encode()).hexdigest()[:6]

def emit(published, forge='unknown', review_url='', posted=0, sugg=0, skipped='', summary='', gate_requested=False, gate_posted=False, gate_skipped_reason='', body_missing=False):
    # The gate travels STRUCTURED, not only folded into the summary string. A
    # required check that never lands blocks the pull request for good, so the
    # run must be able to say so with a predicate rather than with prose a
    # reader has to notice -- publish_health is what raises it.
    # (No backticks in these comments: this block is a backtick-delimited
    # command: string, and one would terminate it. Same trap as below.)
    print(json.dumps({'published': published, 'forge': forge, 'review_url': review_url,
                      'comments_posted': posted, 'suggestions_posted': sugg,
                      'body_missing': bool(body_missing),
                      'skipped_reason': skipped, 'summary': summary,
                      'gate_requested': bool(gate_requested), 'gate_posted': bool(gate_posted),
                      'gate_skipped_reason': gate_skipped_reason}))
    raise SystemExit(0)

url = os.environ.get('PUB_URL', '').strip()
tok = os.environ.get('PUB_TOKEN', '').strip()
# Read HERE, not at the gate block below: every emit() has to be able to say
# whether a gate was expected, and the three failure exits run long before that
# block. Left there, gate_requested defaulted to False on exactly the paths
# where the check did NOT land -- publish_health then reported the publish as
# healthy over a pull request nobody can merge. (No double quotes and no
# backticks in these comments: the body rides a python3 -I -c argument, and either
# one truncates it. TestReviewPRPublishCommandsRunCleanly executes it to check.)
gate_enabled = str(os.environ.get('GATE_ENABLED', '')).strip().lower() in ('1', 'true', 'yes', 'on')
pr = os.environ.get('PR_URL', '').strip()
mode = os.environ.get('MODE', '').strip() or 'inline'
findings_parse_failed = False

# Stale-review guard (#1): the findings' file:line anchors were computed
# against the tree at REVIEWED_SHA. If the worktree HEAD has since moved (a
# resume after the branch advanced), those anchors no longer match — refuse to
# post a misaligned review rather than land comments on the wrong lines.
#
# It only ever fires on two REAL shas that differ. Anything else — an empty
# value, or an unresolved template that reached us as literal text — means the
# guard cannot evaluate itself, and a guard that cannot evaluate itself must
# not swallow the review: the publish carries the gate status, and a status
# that never lands blocks the PR for good.
reviewed = os.environ.get('REVIEWED_SHA', '').strip()
ws = os.environ.get('WS', '').strip()
looks_like_sha = len(reviewed) >= 7 and all(c in '0123456789abcdefABCDEF' for c in reviewed)
stale_anchors = ''
if reviewed and ws and looks_like_sha:
    try:
        cur = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'rev-parse', 'HEAD'],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
    except Exception:
        cur = ''
    if cur and cur != reviewed:
        stale_anchors = reviewed[:12] + ' -> ' + cur[:12]

try:
    findings = json.loads(os.environ.get('FINDINGS') or '[]')
except ValueError:
    findings = []
    findings_parse_failed = True
if not isinstance(findings, list):
    findings = []
    findings_parse_failed = True

# RECOVERY: converge is an LLM step and sometimes emits its findings as prose
# (observed: 'See structured findings array.') instead of the array, which
# would silently drop EVERY finding from the review and fail-close the gate on
# a lie. Fall back to the reviewers own raw arrays, unioned and de-duplicated
# DETERMINISTICALLY by anchor. The LLM merge (cross-confirmation, threshold,
# cap) is lost -- publishing an un-merged set beats publishing nothing.
recovered_from_reviewers = False
if findings_parse_failed:
    union = []
    for env_name in ('CLAUDE_FINDINGS', 'GPT_FINDINGS'):
        try:
            arr = json.loads(os.environ.get(env_name) or '[]')
        except ValueError:
            arr = []
        if isinstance(arr, list):
            union.extend([f for f in arr if isinstance(f, dict)])
    seen = set()
    deduped = []
    for f in union:
        key = (str(f.get('file') or ''), str(f.get('line') or ''),
               str(f.get('title') or '').strip().lower()[:80])
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        deduped.append(f)
    if deduped:
        findings = deduped
        recovered_from_reviewers = True
        findings_parse_failed = False
# QUESTIONS arrives as a newline-separated string (NOT JSON): a json array of
# strings would be space-joined by the tool-command substitution and break this
# shell (a question in command position -> exit 127). One question per line.
questions = [q.strip() for q in (os.environ.get('QUESTIONS') or '').split(chr(10)) if q.strip()]
# TICKET_CONFORMANCE follows the same scalar newline-joined contract.
tickets = [t.strip() for t in (os.environ.get('TICKET_CONFORMANCE') or '').split(chr(10)) if t.strip()]
# WORKSPACE ANOMALY routing (#1666): entries the precheck found uncommitted
# are workspace state, never PR content -- the reviewers were told to scope
# them out, and a finding anchored on one anyway judges an edit the pull
# request does not contain. Strip it from the PR findings and report it
# under the anomaly section below, so the review says the workspace drifted
# instead of blaming the change under review. An unresolved template ref
# reaches the shell as literal braces on a run wired before this input
# existed and must read as a clean tree. The entries are porcelain -z
# fields (never C-quoted, a rename reduced to its NEW path by the
# precheck), one per line: the two status columns and their space come
# off, the path is matched whole -- a quoted or off-by-one name would slip
# past the strip and be posted as a PR finding.
#
# Two shapes are NOT entries. '?' alone: the precheck's status read FAILED
# -- the strip has nothing to work with, and a silent clean tree would
# disarm it without a trace, so the marker turns into a summary line
# instead. And any line without the two status columns and their space:
# the transport is newline-joined, so a filename holding a literal
# newline arrives as its first line (a valid entry, kept -- the residual
# documented at the precheck read) plus an orphan fragment that l[3:]
# would mangle into a phantom path able to reclassify a LEGITIMATE
# finding; the fragment is rejected.
anomaly_raw = os.environ.get('WORKSPACE_ANOMALY') or ''
if anomaly_raw.startswith(chr(123) * 2):
    anomaly_raw = ''
workspace_check_failed = anomaly_raw.strip() == '?'
anomaly_lines = []
if not workspace_check_failed:
    for l in anomaly_raw.split(chr(10)):
        if len(l) > 3 and l[2] == ' ' and l.strip():
            anomaly_lines.append(l)
anomaly_paths = set()
for l in anomaly_lines:
    anomaly_paths.add(l[3:].strip())
# A finding is routed on its ANCHOR, compared whole with an uncommitted
# entry: that equality is what lets the reclassification claim the PR's diff
# does not hold the finding, and what keeps a NAMESAKE of a drifted file --
# 5056 of this tree's 13993 tracked files share a basename -- from greening
# the merge gate over a real defect of the pull request. The anchor's
# RENDERING is normalized away first: the reviewer is handed the workspace as
# an absolute path, so the anchor it writes may carry ./, / or that directory,
# and a diff file header renders the same path as a/ or b/ -- the same file
# under another rendering is the same file. rooted_prefixes holds the three
# rooted ones for this reader and for the matcher below, so neither can
# accept a rendering the other rejects. The name-shaped match the free-text
# channels use is deliberately NOT accepted here: a name cannot tell the
# drifted file from a namesake, and on this channel that ambiguity decides a
# merge, so a bare-basename anchor stays the pull request's -- loud, beside
# the anomaly section that names the drift.
rooted_prefixes = [x for x in [ws.rstrip('/') + '/' if ws.startswith('/') else '', './', '/'] if x]
def in_workspace(p):
    # A path OF this workspace, asked of the workspace itself. Only a
    # repo-relative rendering can be one: an absolute path, or one climbing
    # out through .., names another tree and the forge anchors nothing on it.
    if not ws.startswith('/') or not p or p.startswith('/') or '..' in p.split('/'):
        return False
    return os.path.exists(os.path.join(ws, p))
def anchor_path(raw):
    a = str(raw or '').strip()
    rooted = True
    while rooted:
        rooted = False
        for root in rooted_prefixes:
            if a.startswith(root):
                a = a[len(root):]
                rooted = True
                break
    # git renders every file header of a diff as a/<path> and b/<path>, and
    # rule 2 of REVIEW SCOPE orders the reviewer to read that diff, so an
    # anchor copied out of one carries that prefix. It is dropped only where
    # this workspace does NOT hold the prefixed path and DOES hold -- or
    # reports uncommitted -- the stripped one: a repo owning a top-level a/
    # or b/ keeps its own anchor on the channel that decides a merge, and an
    # anchor the workspace resolves neither way stays as it was written.
    if a[:2] in ('a/', 'b/') and not in_workspace(a) and (a[2:] in anomaly_paths or in_workspace(a[2:])):
        return a[2:]
    return a
anomaly_findings = []
if anomaly_paths:
    kept = []
    for f in findings:
        if isinstance(f, dict) and anchor_path(f.get('file')) in anomaly_paths:
            anomaly_findings.append(f)
        else:
            kept.append(f)
    findings = kept
# The two FREE-TEXT channels carry the same phantom as a finding (#1674): a
# question, or a ticket verdict, that NAMES a drifted entry is about
# workspace state, and published as the pull request's it makes the author
# answer for an edit the pull request does not contain. Neither channel
# carries a file anchor, so the match is on the NAME: an anomaly path, or
# any of its segment suffixes carrying an extension or a directory, because
# a reviewer writes the file the way prose does -- the basename, not its
# three-directory path. Reclassified, never dropped: the text is reprinted
# under the anomaly section below, as a reclassified finding already is, and
# under what the match actually establishes -- that the line names one of
# the uncommitted entries, not that the question is outside the PR's diff.
# The third reviewer-authored free text, the audited perimeter printed in the
# details fold, is filtered by this same matcher where it is printed.
#
# Both boundaries are load-bearing, and they are why this is not a substring
# test. On the LEFT, a name preceded by a word character, a dot, a dash or a
# slash is a DIFFERENT file -- a sibling path ending in the same basename,
# or my-catalog.md when the drift is catalog.md -- whose question must stay
# a question; 548 of this tree's tracked paths end with another tracked path
# (bots/app-dev/README.md ends with README.md), so dropping the slash would
# hand every one of them to a drifted root file. On the RIGHT, a trailing
# word character, slash or dash is likewise another name (catalog.mdx,
# catalog.md-old); a trailing dot is allowed, being how a sentence ends. A
# suffix reduced to a bare extension-less segment is ordinary prose (changes,
# readme) and is never matched.
#
# The whole path also travels ROOTED, and the left exclusion of the slash
# would reject every such form: the reviewer is handed the workspace as an
# absolute path and told to run git -C there, so an absolute rendering of the
# file it just read is ordinary prose, as are ./path and /path. Those three
# prefixes are matched EXPLICITLY -- this workspace's own directory, never an
# arbitrary one -- which keeps the exclusion doing its job on every other
# slash-prefixed rendering. A forge blob URL, whose path segment sits under
# an arbitrary ref, is NOT matched: it is indistinguishable from the 548
# sibling paths above, so it stays a question. A rooted rendering also has to
# OPEN its word -- line start, or after a space, a bracket or a backtick:
# the left exclusion admits every other character before the slash, and
# ~/catalog.md or file:/catalog.md names a file outside this workspace. A
# quote-delimited rooted rendering is the residual of that closed set.
#
# A whole path that is ONE extension-less segment (a root-level build, test
# or run script) is both a path and an ordinary English word. Carrying no
# capital, it is matched only in a rooted rendering, which prose does not
# produce -- a bare one would hand every question using that word to the
# anomaly, and the axis token of a scanned area with it. A capital names the
# file in prose (LICENSE, Makefile, Dockerfile), so that form is matched
# bare too; the test is on the ABSENCE of an uppercase letter, because a
# caseless name (2024) reads as prose just as a lowercase one does.
anomaly_name_res = []
rooted_pre = r'(?:^|(?<=[\s(\[' + chr(96) + r']))(?:' + '|'.join(re.escape(x) for x in rooted_prefixes) + r')'
for p in sorted(anomaly_paths):
    segs = [s for s in p.split('/') if s]
    for i in range(len(segs)):
        cand = '/'.join(segs[i:])
        bare_word = '/' not in cand and '.' not in cand
        if i and bare_word:
            continue
        pre = '' if i else (rooted_pre if bare_word and cand == cand.lower() else r'(?:' + rooted_pre + r')?')
        anomaly_name_res.append(re.compile(r'(?<![\w./-])' + pre + re.escape(cand) + r'(?![\w/-])'))
def names_anomaly(text):
    return any(r.search(text) for r in anomaly_name_res)
anomaly_questions = []
kept_questions = []
for q in questions:
    if names_anomaly(q):
        anomaly_questions.append(q)
    else:
        kept_questions.append(q)
questions = kept_questions
# A ticket verdict is routed only where it would otherwise be CHARGED to the
# pull request. A covered verdict is printed nowhere and an unverifiable one
# keeps its own list in the details fold, so reclassifying either publishes a
# declared ticket gap the reviewer never wrote -- over a verdict that says the
# ticket is covered, on the common run where the catalogue a ticket is
# verified against is the file that drifted.
# The verdict is read off the line's FIRST field: the text up to its first
# colon, or the whole URL when the ref is rendered as one -- a PR body writes
# its Closes line as an issue URL and rule 3 of TICKET CONFORMANCE collects
# refs from there, so a ref carrying :// and a port's second colon has to be
# crossed whole, or its covered line reads as no verdict and publishes as a
# declared ticket gap. The URL alternative is confined to the line's first
# whitespace-free run, so a reason that mentions covered after a later colon
# still reads as the gap it is.
def ticket_verdict(t, verdict):
    return re.match(r'^(?:[^\s:]+://\S+?|[^:]+):\s*' + verdict + r'(?:\s*[—–-]|\s*$)', t, re.I)
anomaly_tickets = []
kept_tickets = []
for t in tickets:
    if names_anomaly(t) and not ticket_verdict(t, 'covered') and not ticket_verdict(t, 'unverifiable'):
        anomaly_tickets.append(t)
    else:
        kept_tickets.append(t)
tickets = kept_tickets
if not url or not tok:
    emit(False, gate_requested=gate_enabled, gate_skipped_reason='no forge publish endpoint bound to this run', skipped='no forge publish endpoint bound to this run: launch through an iterion server with a team forge connection covering the PR repo, so it injects forge_publish_url/forge_publish_token', summary='publish skipped: run holds no server-side forge publish grant (and never uses workspace tokens to post).')

nl = chr(10)
sev_rank = {'critical': 0, 'high': 1, 'medium': 2, 'low': 3}
counts = {}
comments = []
finding_ids = []
unanchored = []
for f in findings:
    if not isinstance(f, dict):
        continue
    sev = str(f.get('severity') or 'low').lower()
    counts[sev] = counts.get(sev, 0) + 1
    cross = str(f.get('reviewers') or '') == 'both'
    fid = finding_id(f)
    finding_ids.append(fid)
    header = fid + ' [' + sev + (' / cross-confirmed' if cross else '') + '] ' + str(f.get('title') or '')
    body = header + nl + nl + str(f.get('detail') or '')
    sketch = str(f.get('suggestion') or '').strip()
    if sketch:
        body = body + nl + nl + 'Suggested fix: ' + sketch
    try:
        line = int(f.get('line') or 0)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        line = 0
    try:
        line_end = int(f.get('line_end') or 0)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        line_end = 0
    repl = str(f.get('replacement') or '')
    # The forge anchors an inline comment on a REPO-RELATIVE path, so the
    # anchor is published under the same normalization the drift test above
    # reads it with: a create-review call is all-or-nothing, and one ./ or
    # workspace-absolute rendering 422s the whole review into the fallback
    # that folds every inline comment into the body. finding_id stays on the
    # RAW anchor -- the engine twin that re-derives it for the fixer's digest
    # hashes the path as written, and normalizing one side alone would break
    # the handle the operator arbitrates by.
    comment = {'path': anchor_path(f.get('file')), 'line': line, 'line_end': line_end,
               'body': body, 'suggestion': repl if repl.strip() else ''}
    if stale_anchors or not comment['path'].strip() or line <= 0:
        unanchored.append(comment)
    else:
        comments.append(comment)

# Keep the visible verdict brief. Detailed findings are posted once, inline;
# preserve the full body here only when no safe inline anchor exists.
if findings_parse_failed:
    summary = ['**Revi — revue inexploitable.**']
elif findings:
    summary = ['**Revi — ' + str(len(findings)) + (' problème à corriger.**' if len(findings) == 1 else ' problèmes à corriger.**'), '',
               ' · '.join(str(counts[sev]) + ' ' + sev for sev in sorted(counts, key=lambda s: sev_rank.get(s, 9)))]
else:
    if anomaly_findings:
        # Never headline a clean review when the strip reclassified some:
        # the verdict is about the PR, and the reclassified count says the
        # workspace drifted — the details ride the anomaly section below.
        summary = ['**Revi — aucun problème à corriger dans la PR ; ' + str(len(anomaly_findings)) + (' finding reclassé' if len(anomaly_findings) == 1 else ' findings reclassés') + ' en anomalie workspace (voir plus bas).**']
    else:
        summary = ['**Revi — aucun problème détecté dans le périmètre revu.**']
if recovered_from_reviewers:
    summary.extend(['', chr(9888) + ' Revue dégradée : findings récupérés des reviewers ; seuil, limite et accord entre modèles non appliqués.'])
elif findings_parse_failed:
    summary.extend(['', chr(9888) + ' Findings illisibles, aucun repli disponible : gate bloquée. Relancer la revue.'])
if stale_anchors:
    summary.extend(['', chr(9888) + ' Le HEAD a changé depuis la revue (' + stale_anchors + '). Findings conservés ci-dessous sans ancrage ; relancer la revue sur la tête actuelle.'])
# Explicit gaps stay visible. Unverifiable context stays available alongside
# the audited scope; a missing ticket is not itself a defect to correct.
ticket_unverified = [t for t in tickets if ticket_verdict(t, 'unverifiable')]
ticket_gaps = [t for t in tickets if not ticket_verdict(t, 'covered') and not ticket_verdict(t, 'unverifiable')]
if ticket_gaps:
    summary.extend(['', '**Écarts au ticket**'])
    summary.extend('- ' + t for t in ticket_gaps)
if questions:
    summary.extend(['', '**À confirmer**'])
    summary.extend('- ' + q for q in dict.fromkeys(questions))
if unanchored:
    summary.extend(['', '**Findings sans ancrage dans le diff**'])
    for c in unanchored:
        anchor = (c['path'] or '(fichier non précisé)') + ':' + str(c['line'])
        summary.extend(['', anchor, '', c['body']])
        if c['suggestion']:
            # This is an unanchored replacement, never an apply-able suggestion.
            fence = chr(96) * (max([len(m[0]) for m in re.finditer(chr(96) + '+', c['suggestion'])] + [2]) + 1)
            summary.extend(['', 'Remplacement proposé :', fence, c['suggestion'], fence])
if anomaly_lines:
    # The workspace is not the pull request's tree: say so, name each drifted
    # entry and the difference, and keep every finding those entries drew out
    # of the PR's findings -- the change under review is not their author.
    summary.extend(['', chr(9888) + ' **Anomalie workspace** — le checkout contient des modifications non commitées que la PR ne porte pas ; rapportées comme état du workspace, jamais comme findings :'])
    summary.extend('- ' + l.strip() for l in anomaly_lines)
    if ws:
        try:
            stat = subprocess.run(['git', '-C', ws, 'diff', '--stat', 'HEAD', '--'] + sorted(anomaly_paths), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30).stdout.strip()
        except Exception:
            stat = ''
        if stat:
            summary.extend(['', 'Différence constatée :', chr(96) * 3, stat, chr(96) * 3])
    for f in anomaly_findings:
        # Rendered like the porcelain entries listed just above, so the reader
        # matches the reclassified finding against the drift that drew it.
        anchor = anchor_path(f.get('file')) + ':' + str(f.get('line') or '')
        summary.extend(['', 'Finding reclassé ici (hors diff de la PR) — ' + anchor + ' — ' + str(f.get('title') or '')])
    # A finding carries its anchor, so its reclassification can claim the
    # PR's diff does not hold it. A free-text line carries only a name: what
    # the match establishes is that the line names one of the entries above,
    # and that is what the label says -- a question about the PR's OWN file
    # whose basename a drifted entry shares lands here too (5056 of this
    # tree's tracked files share a basename), and must not be published as
    # a claim about the pull request's diff.
    for q in dict.fromkeys(anomaly_questions):
        summary.extend(['', 'Question reclassée ici (nomme un fichier non commité listé ci-dessus) — ' + q])
    for t in dict.fromkeys(anomaly_tickets):
        summary.extend(['', 'Écart au ticket reclassé ici (nomme un fichier non commité listé ci-dessus) — ' + t])
if workspace_check_failed:
    # The strip was disarmed by the failed status read: say so rather than
    # letting the review read as if the workspace had been verified clean.
    summary.extend(['', chr(9888) + ' **État du workspace non vérifié** — la lecture git status du precheck a échoué : la présence d\'éditions non commitées hors diff de la PR n\'a pas pu être contrôlée, et aucun finding n\'a été reclassé. Relancer la revue sur un workspace lisible.'])

# Run telemetry is engine-owned: never ask a reviewer to self-report it.
# Effort is expanded by the engine before entering the tool sandbox, using
# the same environment/default as the requested node setting above; it is
# not a provider measurement. Only executed nodes have stamped metadata.
import html
from urllib.parse import urlsplit, urlunsplit, quote

def ai_value(name):
    value = os.environ.get(name, '').strip()
    return '' if value.startswith(chr(123) * 2) or value in ('null', '<nil>', 'None') else value

def ai_tokens(value):
    try:
        count = int(value)
        return format(count, ',').replace(',', ' ') if count >= 0 else 'indisponible'
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return 'indisponible'

def ai_cell(value):
    return html.escape(str(value or 'indisponible'), quote=True).replace('|', '&#124;').replace(chr(10), ' ').replace(chr(13), ' ')

def ai_run_identity():
    run_id = ai_value('AI_RUN_ID')
    label = '<code>' + ai_cell(run_id) + '</code>'
    # The server builds this callback from its public base URL. Use the same
    # ordinary authenticated run page as the merge gate, never a share grant.
    suffix = '/api/v1/forge/publish-review'
    try:
        endpoint = urlsplit(os.environ.get('PUB_URL', ''))
        if (endpoint.scheme not in ('https', 'http') or not endpoint.hostname
                or endpoint.username is not None or endpoint.password is not None
                or not endpoint.path.endswith(suffix)
                or not re.fullmatch(r'[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*', run_id)):
            return label
        # The studio is served under /studio (pkg/deeplink.StudioBase); a bare
        # /runs/<id> still resolves through the server's redirect, but the link
        # published on the pull request should be the address itself.
        path = endpoint.path[:-len(suffix)] + '/studio/runs/' + quote(run_id, safe='')
        run_url = urlunsplit((endpoint.scheme, endpoint.netloc, path, '', ''))
    except ValueError:
        return label
    return '<a href=' + chr(34) + html.escape(run_url, quote=True) + chr(34) + '>' + label + '</a>'

def ai_family(model, wire):
    # The family that SERVED, never the slot's name: a slot's model and
    # backend are dials, and a row titled Revue GPT over a claude model is
    # a lie the reader cannot check against the next column. The session's
    # routing label decides first, because a facade serves whatever id it
    # is sent: z.ai answers a claude id with GLM, and only the label knows.
    # An unrecognised facade claims no family at all rather than trust the
    # model id it was sent. Only the family is published, never the label.
    w = str(wire or '').lower()
    if w.startswith('facade:'):
        parts = w.split(':')
        slot = parts[1] if len(parts) > 2 else ''
        return {'zai': 'GLM', 'moonshot': 'Kimi'}.get(slot, '')
    m = str(model or '').lower()
    if 'glm' in m:
        return 'GLM'
    if 'kimi' in m or 'moonshot' in m:
        return 'Kimi'
    if 'claude' in m or 'fable' in m or m.startswith('anthropic/'):
        return 'Claude'
    if 'gpt' in m or 'codex' in m or m.startswith('openai/'):
        return 'GPT'
    return ''

ai_rows = []
for prefix, glance in [('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE', False), ('AI_REVIEWER_GPT', False), ('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE', True), ('AI_REVIEWER_GPT_GLANCE', True), ('AI_CONVERGE', False)]:
    model = ai_value(prefix + '_MODEL')
    harness = ai_value(prefix + '_BACKEND')
    tokens = ai_value(prefix + '_TOKENS')
    if not (model or harness or tokens):
        continue
    family = ai_family(model, ai_value(prefix + '_WIRE'))
    if prefix == 'AI_CONVERGE':
        label = 'Synthèse' + (' · ' + family if family else '')
    else:
        label = 'Revue' + (' ' + family if family else '') + (' · glance' if glance else '')
    effort = ai_value(prefix + '_EFFORT')
    ai_rows.append('| ' + ' | '.join(ai_cell(v) for v in (label, model, harness, effort, ai_tokens(tokens))) + ' |')
summary.extend(['', '<details>', '<summary><sub>Détails du run IA · Iterion</sub></summary>', '',
                'Run : ' + ai_run_identity(), '',
                'Tokens du run au moment de publier : **' + ai_tokens(ai_value('AI_RUN_TOKENS')) + '**.', ''])
scope = ai_value('REVIEW_SCOPE')
if scope:
    # The third reviewer-authored free text publish prints, and a CLAIM: the
    # perimeter audited. Rule 2d puts a drifted entry out of scope whole, so
    # an area naming one claims a read of a file whose PR state this
    # workspace cannot show -- a facade in the channel built to be
    # falsifiable. Reclassified like a question, never dropped: the area
    # stays printed, under what it is. And under what the match establishes
    # -- the line NAMES one of the entries above, which is all an area
    # carries: the area may well be the pull request's own namesake file,
    # audited, so the published line never claims the file was not reviewed.
    scope_areas = [s.strip() for s in scope.split(' ; ') if s.strip()]
    scope_drifted = [s for s in scope_areas if names_anomaly(s)]
    scope_reviewed = [s for s in scope_areas if not names_anomaly(s)]
    if scope_reviewed:
        summary.extend(['Périmètre revu : ' + ai_cell(' ; '.join(scope_reviewed)), ''])
    if scope_drifted:
        summary.extend(['Périmètre déclaré nommant un fichier non commité listé ci-dessus, donc non publié comme revu : ' + ai_cell(' ; '.join(scope_drifted)), ''])
if ticket_unverified:
    summary.extend(['Vérifications de ticket indisponibles :'] + ['- ' + ai_cell(t) for t in ticket_unverified] + [''])
dual = str(os.environ.get('REVIEW_MODE', '')).strip().lower() == 'dual'
if dual:
    cross_n = len([f for f in findings if isinstance(f, dict) and str(f.get('reviewers') or '') == 'both'])
    summary.extend(['Revue à deux familles ; ' + str(cross_n) + ' finding(s) cross-confirmed by both model families.', ''])
else:
    summary.extend(['Revue mono (single model family).', ''])
fixer_hint = str(os.environ.get('FIXER_HINT', '')).strip()
if finding_ids:
    # The ids gathered in one place, so arbitrating one does not mean
    # scrolling the inline comments for its header. Deduplicated: two findings
    # on the same file and title derive the same id, and a list that repeats
    # one reads as two handles.
    summary.extend(['Identifiants des findings : ' + ', '.join(dict.fromkeys(finding_ids)) + '.', ''])
    if fixer_hint:
        # Only the two characters that can change the document's STRUCTURE:
        # an angle-bracketed word must reach the reader instead of being
        # eaten as a tag, and a stray closing tag must not lift the rest of
        # this block out of its fold. Quotes, pipes and ampersands are left
        # alone — inside a code span a character reference is literal text,
        # and a fixer hint is exactly where an operator quotes a command
        # meant to be copied, shell operators and URL query strings included.
        summary.extend([fixer_hint.replace('{finding}', finding_ids[0]).replace('<', '&lt;').replace('>', '&gt;'), ''])
if ai_rows:
    summary.extend(['| Étape | Modèle | Harness | Effort demandé | Tokens |',
                    '|---|---|---|---|---:|'] + ai_rows)
else:
    summary.append('Détails par étape indisponibles pour ce run.')
summary.extend(['', '<sub>Tokens cumulés des appels IA, pas la taille du contexte. L’effort indiqué est le réglage demandé au modèle.</sub>', '', '</details>'])

payload = {'pr_url': pr, 'summary': nl.join(summary), 'mode': mode, 'comments': comments}

# Deterministic merge-gate verdict: count findings at/above the blocking
# severity floor. The server maps 0 blocking -> success, >0 -> failure on the
# revi/review commit status. Questions never count.
# Resolved unconditionally: the summary below reads it whenever the server
# reports a posted gate, and a NameError in this deterministic final step
# would cost the whole review.
gate_ctx = str(os.environ.get('GATE_CONTEXT', '')).strip() or 'revi/review'
# diff_precheck's file count, carried all the way to the gate. A NON-NEGATIVE
# count is the only shape that means the scope was answered; everything else --
# the -1 sentinel, an empty value, null / <nil> / None, or a template that was
# never substituted -- is the ABSENCE of an answer and fails closed.
#
# A whitelist, deliberately, because the blacklist form fails OPEN on every
# shape it forgot: testing for the literal -1 alone would post success on a
# review that read nothing the moment the value arrived unsubstituted -- which
# is precisely what the first wiring of this mapping produced. ai_value just
# below exists to filter those same renders out of the sibling AI_* mappings,
# so they are not hypothetical in this command.
try:
    scope_unresolved = int(str(os.environ.get('SCOPE_FILES', '')).strip()) < 0
except (ValueError, TypeError):
    scope_unresolved = True
# The engine writes _fallback_used ONLY on a node it did not serve with its
# first choice, so presence is the fact -- read through ai_value, which drops
# an unsubstituted render (the glance twin never runs when topology picked the
# full-strength node, and vice versa). Tested for an explicit true rather than
# truthiness: an unexpected shape reads as no-fallback, which only costs the
# note, never a verdict.
reviewer_fallback = (ai_value('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_FALLBACK').lower() in ('true', '1', 'yes')
                     or ai_value('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_FALLBACK').lower() in ('true', '1', 'yes'))
converge_fallback = ai_value('AI_CONVERGE_FALLBACK').lower() in ('true', '1', 'yes')
fallback_served = reviewer_fallback or converge_fallback
if gate_enabled:
    gate_sev = str(os.environ.get('GATE_SEVERITY', 'high')).strip().lower() or 'high'
    # sev_rank is INVERTED (critical=0 .. low=3), so a SMALLER rank is MORE
    # severe. floor is the rank of the blocking threshold; rank <= floor
    # therefore means at least as severe as the threshold (e.g. floor=high=1
    # blocks critical=0 and high=1, not medium=2/low=3). Unknown rank=0
    # (most severe) always blocks -- fail closed.
    floor = sev_rank.get(gate_sev, 1)
    # A gate fails CLOSED: an unparseable finding set can't be evaluated, so it
    # blocks rather than reporting a green status by omission. The same is true
    # one step earlier -- a scope that never resolved means no code was read at
    # all, and zero findings out of zero files read is not an approval. Measured
    # 2026-09-15: a cloud run whose workspace held no checkout produced 0
    # findings and the reviewers said so in questions, which is a NON-BLOCKING
    # channel by design, so the gate would have gone green on a pull request
    # nobody had opened.
    if scope_unresolved or findings_parse_failed:
        blocking = 1
    else:
        blocking = 0
        for f in findings:
            if not isinstance(f, dict):
                continue
            # An off-enum severity (a trailing-space high, a synonym like
            # blocker or major, anything the model wrote outside the four
            # values) is UNKNOWN, and an unknown severity must block, never
            # silently downgrade to low. NOTE: keep this python -I -c body free of
            # the double-quote character -- the whole body is wrapped by the
            # shell in a double-quoted argument, so a stray one ends the string
            # and truncates the script (a silent empty output, no publish).
            # Same trap as backticks: use plain words in comments here.
            rank = sev_rank.get(str(f.get('severity') or '').strip().lower())
            if rank is None:
                rank = 0
            if rank <= floor:
                blocking += 1
    payload['gate'] = {'enabled': True, 'context': gate_ctx,
                       'blocking_count': blocking, 'threshold': gate_sev,
                       'total_findings': len(findings)}
    # Pin the verdict to the revision it JUDGED. The publish endpoint resolves
    # the pull request head at the moment it writes the status, so a push
    # landing between the review and here would otherwise take this verdict as
    # its own; the server refuses a stale pin instead of retargeting. This is
    # the very sha stale_anchors above already compares against the worktree
    # HEAD - it was reported in the comment and never enforced on the gate.
    if looks_like_sha:
        payload['gate']['audited_sha'] = reviewed
    # Never let the status describe an unreadable review as a real blocking
    # finding: that message sent an operator hunting for a finding that was
    # never published. The note field overrides the server description.
    if scope_unresolved:
        payload['gate']['note'] = 'no diff could be read, so no code was reviewed - gate fails closed; re-run once the workspace carries a checkout'
    elif findings_parse_failed:
        payload['gate']['note'] = 'review output unreadable (findings were not machine-readable) - gate fails closed; re-run the review'
    elif recovered_from_reviewers:
        payload['gate']['note'] = str(blocking) + ' blocking of ' + str(len(findings)) + ' finding(s) recovered from the reviewers (merge step degraded)'
    elif fallback_served:
        # ADVISORY, not fail-closed, and the distinction is deliberate: the
        # three branches above are NON-reviews (no diff read, output
        # unreadable, merge step lost) where a green status would approve
        # by omission. A fallback-served review read the same diff and
        # emitted the same schema; only the model behind it changed. Failing
        # closed here would turn the rescue route into a merge block on
        # exactly the runs it exists to keep reviewing. What must not happen
        # is a silent swap, so the status names it.
        served = []
        if reviewer_fallback:
            served.append('the claude reviewer ran on its fallback route (' + (ai_value('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_MODEL') or ai_value('AI_REVIEWER_CLAUDE_GLANCE_MODEL') or 'unnamed model') + ')')
        if converge_fallback:
            served.append('the merge step ran on its fallback route (' + (ai_value('AI_CONVERGE_MODEL') or 'unnamed model') + ')')
        payload['gate']['note'] = str(blocking) + ' blocking of ' + str(len(findings)) + ' finding(s); ' + ' and '.join(served) + ' because the primary credential could not serve'
    if anomaly_findings:
        # The strip must not read as approval by omission: the gate note
        # says how many findings were reclassified as workspace state, so a
        # green status still shows the workspace drifted.
        extra = str(len(anomaly_findings)) + (' finding' if len(anomaly_findings) == 1 else ' findings') + ' reclassified as workspace anomaly (uncommitted edit outside the PR diff), not counted'
        note = payload['gate'].get('note') or ''
        payload['gate']['note'] = (note + '; ' + extra) if note else str(blocking) + ' blocking of ' + str(len(findings)) + ' finding(s); ' + extra
req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=json.dumps(payload).encode(),
                             headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'X-Iterion-Run': tok},
                             method='POST')
try:
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=120) as resp:
        res = json.loads(resp.read().decode())
except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
    try:
        detail = e.read().decode()[:500].strip()
    except Exception:
        detail = ''
    emit(False, gate_requested=gate_enabled, gate_skipped_reason='forge publish endpoint HTTP ' + str(e.code), skipped='forge publish endpoint HTTP ' + str(e.code) + ': ' + detail, summary='deterministic publish FAILED: the iterion forge-publish endpoint refused the request (HTTP ' + str(e.code) + ').')
except Exception as e:
    emit(False, gate_requested=gate_enabled, gate_skipped_reason='forge publish endpoint unreachable', skipped='forge publish endpoint unreachable: ' + str(e)[:300], summary='deterministic publish FAILED: could not reach the iterion forge-publish endpoint.')

posted = int(res.get('comments_posted') or 0)
sugg = int(res.get('suggestions_posted') or 0)
fallback = str(res.get('fallback') or '')
verified = bool(res.get('verified'))
body_missing = res.get('body_missing') is True
s = 'posted ' + str(posted) + ' inline comment(s), ' + str(sugg) + ' with one-click suggestions, via the iterion forge-publish endpoint (server-side live-token client); counts ' + ('re-fetched from the forge.' if verified else 'as submitted (forge re-fetch unavailable).')
if body_missing:
    s = s + ' review body did not land; only the inline comments reported above can be confirmed.'
elif fallback:
    s = s + ' fallback=' + fallback + ': rejected inline anchors were folded into the review summary body, not dropped.'
if bool(res.get('gate_posted')):
    s = s + ' merge gate: ' + gate_ctx + '=' + str(res.get('gate_state') or '') + ' on ' + str(res.get('gate_sha') or '')[:12] + '.'
elif str(res.get('gate_error') or ''):
    s = s + ' merge gate NOT posted: ' + str(res.get('gate_error')) + '.'
emit(bool(res.get('published')), forge=str(res.get('provider') or 'unknown'),
     review_url=str(res.get('review_url') or ''), posted=posted, sugg=sugg, skipped='', summary=s,
     gate_requested=gate_enabled, gate_posted=bool(res.get('gate_posted')),
     gate_skipped_reason=str(res.get('gate_error') or ''), body_missing=body_missing)
"`
  input: publish_input
  output: publish_output

## Deterministic anti-façade gate (no LLM): when findings existed but
## zero inline comments landed, or the review body did not land, emit a
## loud banner. The board + report already succeeded upstream, so this
## informs (degraded=true) rather than hard-failing the run.
tool publish_health:
  command: `TOTAL={{input.total_findings}} POSTED={{input.comments_posted}} PUBLISHED={{input.published}} BODY_MISSING={{input.body_missing}} REVIEW_URL={{input.review_url}} SKIPPED={{input.skipped_reason}} GATE_REQ={{input.gate_requested}} GATE_POSTED={{input.gate_posted}} GATE_SKIPPED={{input.gate_skipped_reason}} python3 -I -c "
import os, json, sys
total = int(os.environ.get('TOTAL', '0') or '0')
posted = int(os.environ.get('POSTED', '0') or '0')
review_url = os.environ.get('REVIEW_URL', '')
skipped = os.environ.get('SKIPPED', '')
def flag(name):
    return str(os.environ.get(name, '')).strip().lower() in ('1', 'true', 'yes', 'on')
body_missing = flag('BODY_MISSING')
# A required status that never lands blocks the pull request for good, with
# nothing red anywhere to say so - the run would otherwise report 'forge
# publish OK'. This is the SAME condition the inline-anchor guard reports on,
# and it is the more expensive of the two: anchors can be re-posted, a gate
# nobody notices is a PR nobody can merge.
gate_missing = flag('GATE_REQ') and not flag('GATE_POSTED')
degraded = body_missing or (total > 0 and posted == 0) or gate_missing
banners = []
if body_missing:
    banners.append(chr(9888) + ' REVIEW BODY MISSING: review body did not land on the PR; only the inline comments reported by the publish step can be confirmed. Re-run with the same pr_url to publish the summary and any unanchored findings.')
if gate_missing:
    banners.append(chr(9888) + ' MERGE GATE NOT POSTED: the review landed but its required check did not (reason: ' + (os.environ.get('GATE_SKIPPED', '') or 'none given') + '). Until a review posts the status on the CURRENT head, this PR cannot merge; push again or re-run the review on the same pr_url.')
if banners:
    banner = ' '.join(banners)
elif degraded:
    banner = chr(9888) + ' FORGE PUBLISH FAILED: ' + str(total) + ' finding(s) to post but 0 inline comments landed on the PR (reason: ' + (skipped or 'none given') + '). The board + markdown report still succeeded; fix the forge CLI auth and re-run with the same pr_url to publish.'
else:
    banner = 'forge publish OK: ' + str(posted) + ' inline comment(s) posted; review: ' + (review_url or 'n/a')
sys.stderr.write(banner + chr(10))
print(json.dumps({'degraded': degraded, 'banner': banner}))
"`
  input: publish_health_input
  output: publish_health_output

## REVIEW TIERS (#685). Deterministic, no LLM: resolves the concrete
## severity_threshold / max_findings / post_to_board / effective
## review-mode from `review_tier`, honouring an explicit override on any
## of the sentinel-default vars (see each var's own doc above). guard's
## branch reproduces the bot's pre-#685 literal defaults exactly, so an
## unpinned run is byte-identical to before this feature existed.
compute tier_expand:
  output: tier_resolution
  expr:
    severity_threshold: "if(vars.severity_threshold != 'auto', vars.severity_threshold, if(vars.review_tier == 'glance', 'high', if(vars.review_tier == 'audit', 'low', 'medium')))"
    max_findings: "if(vars.max_findings != 0, vars.max_findings, if(vars.review_tier == 'glance', 5, if(vars.review_tier == 'audit', 40, 15)))"
    post_to_board: "if(vars.post_to_board != 'auto', vars.post_to_board == 'true', vars.review_tier != 'glance')"
    effective_review_mode: "if(vars.review_mode == 'dual' || vars.review_tier == 'audit', 'dual', 'mono')"

## Converges the reviewer branch(es) and fills in whichever family did not
## run, so the merging agent sees a stated absence instead of a raw template.
## Reads BOTH a family's full-strength and glance node: topology's condition
## router only ever schedules one of the two per family per run, so the
## `if()` fallback chain below picks up whichever one actually executed.
compute merge_reviews:
  output: family_reviews
  ## Declared as the hand-off fallback when the merged set degrades to prose,
  ## so it has to be persisted too.
  publish: family_findings
  ## Mono reaches this node as an ordinary trunk step, where the await mode
  ## is irrelevant. Dual fires it ONCE, after both reviewers have settled;
  ## best_effort keeps that firing when one reviewer fails and lists the
  ## failure in `_failed_branches` instead of failing the run.
  await: best_effort
  expr:
    review_scope: "join(unique(concat(outputs.reviewer_claude.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_gpt.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.scanned_areas)), ' ; ')"
    claude_ran: "outputs.reviewer_claude.family == 'claude' || outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.family == 'claude'"
    claude_summary: "if(outputs.reviewer_claude.summary, outputs.reviewer_claude.summary, if(outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.summary, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.summary, '(this family did not run)'))"
    claude_findings: "if(outputs.reviewer_claude.findings, outputs.reviewer_claude.findings, if(outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.findings, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.findings, '[]'))"
    claude_areas: "if(outputs.reviewer_claude.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_claude.scanned_areas, if(outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.scanned_areas, ''))"
    claude_questions: "if(outputs.reviewer_claude.questions, outputs.reviewer_claude.questions, if(outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.questions, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.questions, ''))"
    claude_ticket_conformance: "if(outputs.reviewer_claude.ticket_conformance, outputs.reviewer_claude.ticket_conformance, if(outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.ticket_conformance, outputs.reviewer_claude_glance.ticket_conformance, ''))"
    gpt_ran: "outputs.reviewer_gpt.family == 'gpt' || outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.family == 'gpt'"
    gpt_summary: "if(outputs.reviewer_gpt.summary, outputs.reviewer_gpt.summary, if(outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.summary, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.summary, '(this family did not run)'))"
    gpt_findings: "if(outputs.reviewer_gpt.findings, outputs.reviewer_gpt.findings, if(outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.findings, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.findings, '[]'))"
    gpt_areas: "if(outputs.reviewer_gpt.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_gpt.scanned_areas, if(outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.scanned_areas, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.scanned_areas, ''))"
    gpt_questions: "if(outputs.reviewer_gpt.questions, outputs.reviewer_gpt.questions, if(outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.questions, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.questions, ''))"
    gpt_ticket_conformance: "if(outputs.reviewer_gpt.ticket_conformance, outputs.reviewer_gpt.ticket_conformance, if(outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.ticket_conformance, outputs.reviewer_gpt_glance.ticket_conformance, ''))"

## ─── Forge PR review (v0.3.0) nodes ─────────────────────────────

## Deterministic branch (no LLM): reach the forge publish step ONLY
## when a pr_url was given. Passes the merged findings + counts through
## so publish_review reads them as input.* (stable across the edge).
##
## It is also the ONE node both merge twins feed, so it is where the merge's
## payload AND its engine telemetry are read from (#2467): whichever twin
## ran maps its own output onto this node's input, and every downstream
## reader asks pr_gate instead of naming a twin — a reader naming one sees
## the empty string on every run the other served, and the published run
## table would describe a merge step that did not run
## (bots/review_pr_mono_gpt_merge_test.go).
compute pr_gate:
  input: pr_gate_input
  output: pr_gate_output
  ## The hand-off's source (manifest `produces:`): the merged set as the
  ## run's one review, written by the node every path crosses rather than
  ## by whichever twin merged it. Same field names the resolver reads —
  ## findings, questions, total_findings.
  publish: merged_review
  expr:
    has_pr: "input.pr_url != ''"
    pr_url: "input.pr_url"
    findings: "input.findings"
    questions: "input.questions"
    ticket_conformance: "input.ticket_conformance"
    total_findings: "input.total_findings"
    claude_findings: "input.claude_findings"
    gpt_findings: "input.gpt_findings"
    ## The merge step's engine telemetry, carried under ONE set of names:
    ## the published run table names the model, backend, token count,
    ## rescue stamp and routing label of the merge that actually ran.
    ai_converge_model: "input.ai_converge_model"
    ai_converge_backend: "input.ai_converge_backend"
    ai_converge_tokens: "input.ai_converge_tokens"
    ai_converge_fallback: "input.ai_converge_fallback"
    ai_converge_wire: "input.ai_converge_wire"
